  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/bf1ba7913469cf12969f8c0fa546f46b881bd38794c1b7b3d2f7500859d6ad56.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(3,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=3.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{b}{3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\ b & =\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t.\n$$",
